<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  org.nextbsd.getty.plist

  The one login getty. It picks exactly one terminal per boot:

    1. The loader was asked for a serial console
       (console contains comconsole, or boot_serial=YES)
         -> getty on /dev/console, which the kernel bound to the UART.
            This is CI, and any serial-console server.
    2. Otherwise, if there is a screen (/dev/ttyv0 exists)
         -> getty on ttyv0, unless Gershwin's loginwindow job is loaded
            (see below).
    3. Otherwise (headless, no framebuffer)
         -> getty on /dev/console.

  Why it replaces the two jobs that were here (org.nextbsd.getty.console
  and org.nextbsd.getty.ttyv0):
    /dev/console binds to exactly ONE tty: cnadd() points it at the first
    console and cnselect() moves the chosen one to the head of cn_devlist
    (sys/kern/kern_cons.c). Which tty that is depends on the machine:

      arm64 with a screen: the EFI loader publishes hw.uart.console from
        ACPI SPCR without being asked, so /dev/console is the UART and the
        screen got no login unless a second job served ttyv0.
      amd64 laptop: /dev/console IS ttyv0, so both jobs targeted the same
        tty. The second one's login_tty() failed with EPERM (the tty
        already belonged to the first job's session, sys/kern/tty.c
        TIOCSCTTY), getty logged "login_tty /dev/ttyv0: Operation not
        permitted", exited, and KeepAlive respawned it every 10 seconds,
        forever.

    Deciding from the loader's own settings covers both with one job: a
    serial console is only preferred when someone asked for it, and
    otherwise the login goes where a person is looking. Measured on an
    amd64 ThinkPad T460s and an arm64 virt VM: one job, getty on v0, no
    log spam; with boot_serial=YES set in kenv the job moves to the UART.

  Automatic login (nextbsd/nextbsd-userland#278):
    /usr/libexec/nextbsd/autologin-user prints an account name when, and
    only when, exactly one user exists, it is "admin", and it has no
    password. When it does, and /etc/gettytab actually defines the class,
    getty runs with nextbsd-autologin, whose "al" capability makes getty
    exec login -f and skip the prompt. Both conditions are checked because
    either half can be absent: the helper ships in the userland package and
    the class in nextbsd-overlays, so an image may have one without the
    other, and the fallback is always the ordinary login prompt.

    The home is built here, synchronously, before getty is exec'd. Automatic
    login happens the instant getty starts, so a one-shot job racing it would
    lose about as often as not, and login would report "No home directory.
    Logging in with home = /" on a first boot. createhomedir is idempotent
    and skips a home that exists, so this costs one stat on every later boot.
    Repairing every user's home, rather than this one account's, is what
    createhomedir -a is for, run by hand.

    The class must inherit 3wire.9600 rather than FreeBSD's stock autologin
    entries, which chain to std.9600 and hardcode al=root. The 3wire
    settings are load-bearing here: nc (no carrier) is what stopped the
    arm64 pl011 hanging after login (nextbsd#355).

  Why ttyv0 is there early enough on arm64:
    vt(4) attaches with the kernel's own virtio_gpu / efifb driver before
    launchd starts; the DRM kext loaded later only replaces the driver
    ("VT: Replacing driver ... with new drmfb"), not the terminal.

  Gershwin loginwindow (nextbsd/nextbsd#540):
    When io.github.gershwin-desktop.loginwindow is loaded in PID 1, the
    graphical login owns the screen and there is no text getty on the video
    console at all, as on OS X 10.9, where the console getty is off in
    /etc/ttys and launchd starts loginwindow. "Loaded" is the Darwin
    mechanism: launchctl load -w / unload -w and the overrides database
    decide whether it is enabled. launchctl loads every LaunchDaemon before any of
    them starts (load -D all), so the loginwindow job is already loaded when
    this one runs.
    The job waits instead of exiting, because KeepAlive would otherwise
    respawn it every 10 seconds. If loginwindow is unloaded later, the
    ttyv0 getty comes back within a minute.
    The serial branches come first and are unchanged, so a serial console
    (and CI) always gets its getty. Headless machines are unchanged too.

  A serial login on a machine that also has a screen:
    set boot_serial="YES" (or console="comconsole") in /boot/loader.conf.

  Why 3wire.9600 (nextbsd#355):
    FreeBSD's std.* gettytab classes lack nc (no carrier), so on a UART
    without DCD the post-login shell hangs opening /dev/tty. 3wire.9600
    sets CLOCAL. On vt(4) carrier always asserts, so it is harmless there.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>org.nextbsd.getty</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/sh</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>cls=3wire.9600; u=$(/usr/libexec/nextbsd/autologin-user 2>/dev/null) &amp;&amp; [ -n "$u" ] &amp;&amp; grep -q '^nextbsd-autologin' /etc/gettytab 2>/dev/null &amp;&amp; { /usr/sbin/createhomedir -u "$u" &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1; cls=nextbsd-autologin; }; case "$(kenv -q console)" in *comconsole*) exec /usr/libexec/getty "$cls" console ;; esac; case "$(kenv -q boot_serial)" in [Yy][Ee][Ss]) exec /usr/libexec/getty "$cls" console ;; esac; if test -c /dev/ttyv0; then while /bin/launchctl list io.github.gershwin-desktop.loginwindow &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1; do sleep 60; done; exec /usr/libexec/getty "$cls" ttyv0; fi; exec /usr/libexec/getty "$cls" console</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
